Give ruby's Haiku stack-overflow handler the arm64 register names.

REAL FIX, not a cut. Nothing is disabled and no declared dependency changes; the
handler keeps working, it just reads the registers that exist on this machine.

## The failure

    signal.c:870:36: error: 'mcontext_t' {aka 'const struct vregs'} has no member
                     named 'esp'; did you mean 'sp'?
    signal.c:871:34: error: 'mcontext_t' {aka 'const struct vregs'} has no member
                     named 'ebp'
    make: *** [Makefile:468: signal.o] Error 1

## Why

`check_stack_overflow()` compares the faulting address against the stack and frame
pointers taken out of the signal's `mcontext_t`, and reads them per platform. The
`__HAIKU__` arm of that `#if` was written when Haiku was x86-only and then extended
once, for x86_64:

    # elif defined __HAIKU__
    #   if defined(__amd64__)
        const unsigned long sp = mctx->rsp;
        const unsigned long bp = mctx->rbp;
    #   else
        const unsigned long sp = mctx->esp;
        const unsigned long bp = mctx->ebp;
    #   endif

so every non-amd64 Haiku architecture falls through to the **x86** names. On arm64
`mcontext_t` is `struct vregs` from `headers/posix/arch/arm64/signal.h`:

    struct vregs {
        ulong       x[30];
        ulong       lr;
        ulong       sp;
        ulong       elr;
        ulong       spsr;
        __uint128_t fp_q[32];
        u_int       fpsr;
        u_int       fpcr;
    };

There is no `esp` and no `ebp`. The stack pointer is `sp`, and the frame pointer on
AArch64 is **x29**, i.e. `x[29]` — note that `x[]` holds x0..x29, so `lr` (x30) is a
separate member and `x[29]` really is the FP rather than an off-by-one.

## The fix

Add an `__aarch64__` arm to that same `#if` rather than rewriting the `#   else`
lines, so the x86 and x86_64 Haiku builds are bit-for-bit untouched:

    # elif defined __HAIKU__
    #   if defined(__amd64__)
        const unsigned long sp = mctx->rsp;
        const unsigned long bp = mctx->rbp;
    #   elif defined(__aarch64__)
        const unsigned long sp = mctx->sp;
        const unsigned long bp = mctx->x[29];
    #   else
        const unsigned long sp = mctx->esp;
        const unsigned long bp = mctx->ebp;
    #   endif

Applied from the recipe's `BUILD()` with a `sed` whose address range is the
`# elif defined __HAIKU__` ... `# endif` block, so it cannot touch the Darwin arm
(which also spells `esp`/`ebp`, via `MCTX_SS_REG`) or the FreeBSD arm (`mc_esp`).

**The edit is verified by content, not by sed's exit code** — `sed` returns 0 when it
matches nothing, which is precisely how this would silently fail to apply after any
upstream reformatting:

    grep -q 'mctx->x\[29\]' signal.c || { echo "FATAL: ..."; exit 1; }

## Why this port matters here

`ruby` is not optional decoration on the way to a browser. It gates `vim`, and `vim`
is the only affordable provider of `cmd:xxd`, which `netsurf-3.11` build-requires.
The tree's only other `cmd:xxd` provider is `qvim-8.0.197`, which wants
`devel:libQt5Core` and `devel:libQt5Gui`.

## The other thing that had to be true first

Before this compile error was even reachable, ruby died at its *first* `make` with

    runtime_loader: Cannot open file libroot.so (needed by /boot/system/bin/make)

because the recipe does `export LIBRARY_PATH=$LIBRARY_PATH:%A` — written assuming
prepend semantics — and Haiku's `runtime_loader` used to *replace* the search path.
That is a platform defect, fixed separately in
`runtime_loader: make LIBRARY_PATH and ADDON_PATH additive`, and it needs a host
carrying that fix. The recipe was always right about that part; do not add a
workaround for it.

Applies to: haikuports/dev-lang/ruby/ruby-3.2.9.recipe (tree copy -- ruby has no
input source package, so the tree recipe is the operative one)

--- a/ruby-3.2.9.recipe
+++ b/ruby-3.2.9.recipe
@@ BUILD()
 BUILD()
 {
+	# graviton: ruby's stack-overflow handler reads the faulting SP and FP out of
+	# mcontext_t. Its __HAIKU__ branch (signal.c, "elif defined __HAIKU__") handles
+	# only __amd64__ and falls through to the x86 names esp/ebp for everything else,
+	# so on arm64 it fails to compile:
+	#   signal.c:870: error: 'mcontext_t' {aka 'const struct vregs'} has no member
+	#                 named 'esp'; did you mean 'sp'?
+	# Haiku's arm64 struct vregs (headers/posix/arch/arm64/signal.h) is
+	# { ulong x[30]; ulong lr; ulong sp; ulong elr; ulong spsr; ... }, so the stack
+	# pointer is sp and the frame pointer is the AArch64 FP, x29 == x[29]. Add an
+	# __aarch64__ arm to that #if rather than rewriting the x86 lines, so the x86
+	# and x86_64 builds are untouched. This is a portability fix, not a cut: the
+	# handler keeps working, it just reads the right registers.
+	sed -i '/^# elif defined __HAIKU__/,/^# endif/{
+		s|^#   else$|#   elif defined(__aarch64__)\n    const unsigned long sp = mctx->sp;\n    const unsigned long bp = mctx->x[29];\n#   else|
+	}' signal.c
+	# Verify by content, never by sed's exit code -- sed returns 0 when it matches
+	# nothing, which is exactly how this edit would silently not happen.
+	grep -q 'mctx->x\[29\]' signal.c || { echo "FATAL: aarch64 mcontext arm not inserted"; exit 1; }
+	grep -n -A2 'elif defined(__aarch64__)' signal.c | head -8
+
 	CFLAGS="-O2 -g -D_BSD_SOURCE" \
 	LDFLAGS="-lbsd" \
 	runConfigure --omit-dirs binDir ./configure \
